Emacsvox News -- History Of User-Visible Changes

Maintained by Emacsvox Contributors.

* Emacsvox Unreleased --- Current User-Visible Changes

Released notes are preserved in [[file:NEWS-2026.10.0][NEWS for Emacsvox 2026.10.0]].

** Before you upgrade

Emacsvox requires Emacs 30.2 or newer and Transient 0.13.3 or newer. Emacs
30.2's bundled Transient is too old for the settings menus.

The supported Omnivox baseline is now 1.18.0, which supplies the expanded
tone controls. Update Omnivox separately; updating Emacsvox does not replace
an existing speech server. Guided installers use matching core and engine
versions.

** Settings menus and search

=C-e H= brings together speech, voices, sounds, pronunciation, feedback
rules and diagnostics. Find a setting with =C-e H /= or an action with
=C-e H a=, using any part of its name. =C-e S= opens settings for the current
application, including Agent Shell, EAT, EWW, Notmuch and Org, or general
buffer settings when no application menu exists.

Menus show whether a change affects this buffer or saved defaults. You can
also review changed settings, manage saved setups and resume unfinished
drafts. Aural Home remains available with =M-x emacsvox-aural=; its old
settings-menu entry has been removed.

** Menu navigation and review

Use arrows to browse settings and Enter to activate the selected item.
Choices explain their effect before you select them. You can review an
item word by word or character by character, and choose whether movement
wraps within a section, stops at its edges or continues across sections.
Returning from Help, a submenu or exact number entry restores the control
you used. Adjustments interrupt older announcements so you hear the current
value and when you reach a limit.

** Speech and voice controls

Speech rate and punctuation menus let you try buffer settings or save
global defaults. Focused voice tuning offers previews, comparisons and undo
for a voice's shared adjustments or one of its fallbacks. You can shorten
comparison labels and adjust the overall pitch contrast between voices.

Settings also expose typing feedback, Voice Lock, local silence, earcons,
notification routing and output volumes. Menu and input feedback remain
audible when ordinary reading is quiet. Pronunciation dictionaries now load
correctly on Emacs 31.

Long passages start speaking sooner in sessions with many loaded packages.

An optional delivery recorder captures one main or notification presentation
for offline investigation. Use =M-x emacsvox-aural-record-next-delivery=,
then inspect its status and explicitly export or cancel it. Captures are
bounded, can contain speech text and paths, and do not enable logging.
=M-x emacsvox-aural-reproduce-delivery= checks a saved capture without
speaking it; a match confirms client construction, not audible playback.

With a supporting Windows Omnivox development build, output settings offer
separate physical devices for main and notification speech. Browse Transient
choices with arrows and Enter to select without changing audio. Apply without
restarting engines, then Save the applied choice
for future sessions. Missing explicit devices never fall back to another output;
recovery retains the confirmed choice. Other backends keep their existing routes.

When notification output is unavailable, command errors and Quit feedback respect
the device fallback policy. They no longer create abandoned main-speech workers
that can block later recovery.

Supporting local Omnivox workers also apply notification Left, Right and Both
channels immediately, including tones and earcons. Main output retires only the
notifier; choosing a separate route starts it without restarting main speech.
Save separately to keep the route for startup. Older workers and other backends
retain their restart controls.

Voice Workbench keeps the latest Apply result when older requests finish.
Immediate failures are no longer announced as pending, and failed processes
retain their original speech-lane labels. Errors in status hooks no longer
prevent Apply or withhold its final result. Saved profiles and unsaved drafts
remain available for recovery.

** Reading filters

Speech settings now let you edit repeated punctuation, ignored columns and
where text is split for speech, with previews and session undo. Column
filters can be saved for later visits to a file. Character speed and
nonprinting-character controls are also available, along with an editor
for character sets to leave unchanged during Unicode conversion. These
settings affect speech without changing the document.

** Wrapped-line reading

=C-e H s R w= lets you choose whether ordinary Up and Down read a whole
source line or one wrapped screen row. Preview a choice, apply it to the
buffer, or save mode-specific or global defaults. Optional sounds and
spoken labels distinguish the first, middle and last rows of a wrapped
line. =C-e l= still reads the whole source line; terminal and table
navigation retain their own behaviour.

** Tone editing

Capitalisation, indentation, spelling and tree feedback share a tone editor.
Adjust pitch, duration, volume, waveform, pitch sweeps and how a tone starts
and fades. Preview the sound before keeping it, and save combinations as
presets. Spelling and indentation tones can play before or alongside speech;
recorded feedback cues also offer after-speech timing.

** Recording library and sound packs

=C-e H o l= opens a library for browsing, importing and previewing recordings.
Assign recordings to cues in a personal sound pack; saving copies the audio
into the pack so moving the original does not break the assignment. The
bundled Accent pack adds another set of feedback sounds.

Library browsing can play recordings before, alongside or after their names,
or leave previewing to you. Alongside is the default; before and after
require Omnivox.

** Indentation feedback

Indentation tones now play alongside speech by default on supporting speech
servers. Thanks to Arkadiusz Świętnicki for contributing this behaviour.
=C-e d I= opens settings for spoken or tonal indentation, pitch changes with
depth, timing and volume. Preview changes in the current buffer or save
global defaults. Spoken-only indentation feedback is unchanged.

** Spelling feedback

Flyspell cues now occur at the misspelt word during reading. Moving into a
flagged word gives its cue; moving within it does not repeat the cue.
Opening a mail reply no longer triggers a burst of spelling sounds.

=C-e H s f= lets you combine voice changes, tones and earcons, preview a
sample sentence and save your preferences. Turning off these cues leaves
spell checking enabled.

** Table settings and HTML mail tables

=C-e H s T= opens shared table settings for Markdown, Org, Agent Shell,
Notmuch HTML mail and supported application lists. Choose row or cell
speech, titles, coordinates and automatic or on-demand entry. Preview
choices before applying them, and save common or mode-specific defaults.

Notmuch can now read recognised HTML data tables in place, including
wrapped cells, while retaining link and attachment actions. Automatic entry
is the default there and in Agent Shell; other readers start on demand.
Control-Meta arrows enter reading explicitly. Pressing =q= leaves reading
in place, and redraws respect that choice. Layout tables and merged cells
in mail retain ordinary navigation.

** Text trees in Agent Shell

Recognised trees in plain-text code blocks read as item labels without the
drawing characters. Entry, exit and changes of level have separate cues;
moving between siblings does not repeat the level. You can jump between
trees through =C-c ]= and =C-c [= by choosing Tree. Copying retains the
original characters; ambiguous diagrams keep ordinary reading.

=C-e S Y= opens Trees settings. Combine spoken levels in a chosen voice,
earcons and tones whose pitch follows depth. Entry and exit have their own
choices. Edit the depth tone and try your changes by moving through
a sample tree with arrow keys, then apply them to the buffer or session,
or save them for later sessions.

** Choose your Muggles menus

Muggles are small navigation and editing menus with single-key commands.
=C-e H m= lets you inspect, enable, disable and run them individually, then
save your choices. The list covers Org headings and tables, text, Outline
and View navigation, Repeatable yank, Info summary and Smartparens. Hydra
is required; the Smartparens menu also needs Smartparens mode.

Personal bindings are preserved. Enable Repeatable yank and Info summary
explicitly to use =C-y=, =M-y= and Info's =?=; loading the older Muggles
collection no longer takes over those standard keys by itself.

** Browse at the Agent Shell prompt

Emacsvox's Explicit input activation setting keeps navigation letters
available when you reach the live prompt. Press Enter there to start
editing; leaving input returns to browsing and keeps your draft. Choose
this through =C-e S i=. Automatic input activation remains the default.
Choose spoken or sound cues for entering and leaving editing.

** Less repetition in Agent Shell questions

In active question forms, Tab reads the question when you enter a field,
then reads each choice and its state without repeating the question. Enter
confirms the choice without repeating its heading. Returning to the field
reads its heading again. Automatic hints for
questions, permission choices, links and folded headings no longer speak
over navigation feedback. Question forms remain an optional experimental
Agent Shell feature.

When expired question controls remain in the transcript, Tab retains their
field-heading context and identifies them as expired. Historical navigation
does not reactivate the form or offer instructions to submit an answer.

** Agent Shell queue and group controls

With Agent Shell 0.94.1 or newer, =M-RET= at an empty prompt can redirect the
last queued prompt into a running turn, including while browsing. With the
default routes, Agent Shell asks before sending it; the agent must support
steering. An unsent draft remains protected.

=C-u RET= on a foldable fragment toggles its whole group and children.
Emacsvox gives one visibility indication for the affected group. These
controls need 0.94.1; the integration's minimum remains 0.85.3.

** Interactive terminal questions

EAT recognises supported arrow-choice menus and announces the selected
option, along with unanswered questions that follow. Output reading resumes
after menu redraws and blank terminal padding. Horizontal drawing characters
in separators are omitted from speech, and submitting input interrupts earlier
terminal output.

** Editing and navigation feedback

Cutting and copying announce the region count promptly and interrupt older
speech. Copying no longer waits for the visual blink. Magit reads the
destination after staging or unstaging, and closing a buffer announces the
buffer you return to, even when related buffers close together.

Org agenda time rows omit decorative characters, and Markdown heading
underlines no longer interrupt navigation with a speech error. Notmuch
HTML links speak correctly, and attachment buttons also work from nearby
whitespace.

** Tutorial and practice document

The manual adds a guided audio-desktop tutorial with a practice document.
Exercises cover reading, editing, settings and returning to your work.
Task lookup and command reminders help you find the relevant instructions,
and the settings menu links directly to the manual.
